## A B S T R A C T
The serious volume changes during the alloy/dealloy process and poor electronic conductivity always limit antimony (Sb) development as Li/Na-ion batteries anode. In this work, we chose metal-organic frameworks (MOFs, MIL-88) as template and successfully synthesized hierarchical tiny-Sb encapsulated in MOFs derivedcarbon and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow nanotubes (Sb⊂CTHNs) by annealing reduction and galvanic replacement methods. The hybridization of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ like a holder maintain the framework of MIL-88 and the advantages of hollow inner nanostructure, intrinsic porosity and high specific surface area were delivered to Sb⊂CTHNs gradually. Tiny-Sb nanoparticles fully embedded in tight carbon matrix and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ walls with ultrastable structure could buffer the volume expansion, enhanced $\mathrm{{Li^{+}/N a^{+}}}$ diffusion and presented superior electrochemical performance as Li/Naion battery anode. For Li-ion battery, Sb⊂CTHNs delivered a reversible capacity of $607.2~\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles at a current density of $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ and ultrahigh rate capability $(434.8~\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at $5000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1};$ . Combined with $\mathrm{LiMn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ cathode, these $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cells also displayed superior capacity and long cycle life. For Na-ion battery, Sb⊂CTHNs exhibited a high initial reversible capacity of $640.8\mathrm{\mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and high rate capability (374.1 mA h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at $5000\ \mathrm{\mA\\g^{-1})}$ . Combined with $\mathrm{Na_{3}V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3}/A C}$ cathode, Sb⊂CTHNs// $\mathrm{{Na_{3}V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3}}}$ full cells also displayed excellent electrochemical performances.
## 1. Introduction
In the 21th century, the special significance of energy is encouraging our human to search greener, more efficient and higher energy density storage devices. Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), as a classical commer­ cialized rechargeable battery, have been developed rapidly and widely used in our daily life [1–5]. However, nowadays the present limited energy density of commercialized LIBs can’t satisfy the urgent market demand of high-tech multifunctional electric vehicles and electronic devices which has attracted a lot of researchers’ attentions [6]. The widely used commercial graphite, as one of the insertion/extraction reaction type anode, confront the serious bottleneck of low specific ca­ pacity $(372\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and inferior rate capability arising from the $\mathrm{LiC}_{6}$ intercalation compound formation and low ${\mathbf{}}{\mathbf{}}{\mathbf{}}^{+}$ diffusion coefficient, which was considered would be replaced by the other reaction types anodes for increasing the LIBs energy density [7–10]. Similar to LIBs energy storage device, Sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) also faced the same challenges. While, Antimony (Sb), one of the alloying/dealloying type
LIBs and SIBs anode, has become one of the next potential anode candidate deriving from its high theoretical capacity of $660\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ or $1750\mathrm{Ah\L^{-1}}$ (approximate two times higher than commercial graphite), stable working voltage $(0.8\mathrm{-}0.9\mathrm{~V~}$ vs ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ , $0.5\mathrm{-}0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{{Na}^{+})}$ , low elec­ trode polarization (approximate $0.2\mathrm{V}_{\cdot}$ , which can avoid the generation of dendritic lithium crystals at the stable working voltage) and unique layer structure [11–14]. Furthermore, the unique layer structure could also benefit the $\mathrm{{Li^{+}/N a^{+}}}$ fast diffusion with the result of increasing the rate capability and higher transfer kinetics [12]. Nevertheless, similar to the other alloying/dealloying anodes of Sn and Si, Sb anode also faced the same great challenge because of the huge volume expansion ( $150\%$ vs LIBs, $390\%$ vs SIBs) due to the transformation of Sb to $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}$ or ${\mathrm{Na}}_{3}{\mathrm{Sb}}$ during the $\mathrm{{Li}^{+}/\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}}$ inserts the host [12,15–17]. Such dramatic volume change can crinkle the solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) film formation on the active materials surface even negative checking and also with the result of anode particles pulverization after several $\mathrm{{Li}^{+}/\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}}$ insertions/ extractions, further to induce the losing electrical contact from current collector and the capacity degradation [8–12,18–20]. Recently, extensive strategies have been researched for the challenges, like the particles nanocrystallization, constructing $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}S\mathbf{b}$ alloys $(\mathbf{M}=S\mathbf{n}$ , Zn and Ni, etc.) and hybridized with carbon or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ materials [11–15,20–23]. The introduction of M can provide a mechanical buffer to mitigate the volume expansion caused by active Sb particles. While, on the other hand, the high molecular wright of M decrease laterally the gravimetric energy density [13,22,23]. Besides, combined with the traditional car­ bon materials, the cycle stability of Sb anodes have been improved, while the phenomenon of huge volume expansion still remained and threaten the battery security. The particles nanocrystallization is also an efficiency method to remit the drastic volume change and the nano­ particles encapsulated in carbon materials which act a role of protector are considered as the next generation methods to utilize in commercial battery [10,11,14,18,24]. Fortunately, the metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) composed of metal ions as network nodes and organic ligands as linkers, came into our sight in recent years with the advantages of unique intrinsic porosity, hollow nanostructure and high surface area and have been widely applied in energy storage, magnetic materials, catalytic activity and biomedical [9,10,13,25–27]. According to the hollow nanostructure and organic ligand, Yoon et al. have synthesized the mesoporous silicon hollow nanocubes derived from the MOFs as LIBs anode which displayed a high reversible capacity of $1728\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ with initial Coulombic efficiency of $80.1\%$ and outstanding rate capacity [9]. Liu’s group also successfully synthesized $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ nanoparticles embedded in hollow graphitic carbon nanocages, MOFs-derived metal selenides, ilmenite ${\mathrm{FeTiO}}_{3}$ hollow nanorods which all displayed superior specific capacity and long cycle life [10,28,29]. For the Sb anode, NiSb alloy embedded in carbon hollow spheres (NiSb⊂CHSs) with stable hollow nanostructure were also prepared and presented excellent electro­ chemical performance. While, the non-electroactive Ni in NiSb alloy vastly negative affect the specific capacity and delivered a $447.2\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ for NiSb⊂CHSs $(667.4\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for pure Sb) at the current density of $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [13]. Therefore, combined pure Sb metal with the MOFs is an urgent need to develop Sb anode for LIBs and SIBs. Herein, we partic­ ularly designed and successfully synthesized MOFs-derived Sb nano­ particles encapsulated in carbon and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow nanotubes (Sb⊂CTHNs) via simple tetrabutyl titanate (TBOT) coating, annealing reduction and galvanic replacement routes. The import of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ like a holder support the framework and allowed Sb⊂CTHNs inherit the MOFs advantages of intrinsic porosity, high specific surface area and stable hollow nanostructure, which can reduce $\mathrm{{Li}^{+}/\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}}$ diffusion and electron transport distance and with the result of displaying excellent rate capability. The nanocrystallized Sb particles encapsulated in carbon matrix and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ could support the volumetric change and the hollow structure also provide sufficient void space to buffer volumetric expansion as the alloying/delloying of Sb, and thus present stable structure, stable SEI film and outstanding cycle stability. Besides, the import of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the current nanoconfined galvanic replacement route can as a typical effort for the materials with unstable structure or diffi­ culty in synthesis.
