2.3. Electrochemical Measurements.
The electrochemical properties of these obtained materials were assessed by using CR2016/CR2032 coin cells at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The active ma­ terial (Sb⊂CTHNs), acetylene black, and polymer binder (poly­ vinylidene fluoride, PVDF) at a mass ratio of 80:10:10 dispersed in 1- Methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) solvent. Then the slurry was coated on current collector (copper foil) by a doctor blade and dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight. The typical mass loading of Sb⊂CTHNs in discs was controlled at $1{-}1.5~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ . In the LIBs half cells, the electrodes were assembled with Li metal was as counter electrode, Celgard 2400 membrane as separator and $1\mathrm{MLiPF}_{6}$ dissolved in Ethylene carbonate (EC) and Diethyl carbonate (DEC) $(1:1,{\mathrm{Vol}}\%)$ with $5\mathrm{vol\%}$ addition of Fluoroethylene carbonate (FEC) as the electrolyte in Ar-filled glove box. The electrochemical properties of the cells were evaluated by Cyclic voltammetry at a scan rate of $0.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ from 0.01 to $2.5\mathrm{V}$ The elec­ trochemical performances of the batteries were performed on a WCBS3000 (WonATech, Korea) battery-test system. For the synthesized $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ half cells, the electrode was composed similar with Sb⊂CTHNs just replaced the cathode active materials (synthesized $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}.$ and the current collector (Al foil). The cutoff voltage of these half cells were measured from $3.2\mathrm{V}$ to $4.5\mathrm{V}$ . The devices of full cells were assembled with the Sb⊂CTHNs as anode electrode and the $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ cathode as counter electrode directly with decreasing the mass loading of Sb⊂CTHNs in discs to $0.5{-}0.7\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and increasing the mass loading of $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ in discs to $6.5{-}7.5~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ . The test voltage of these full cells was measured from $2.0\mathrm{V}$ to $4.0\mathrm{V}$ . For SIBs, the batteries applied Sodium foil as counter electrode, glass microfibers (GF/D, Whatman) as separator and $1.0\mathrm{~M~NaClO}_{4}$ in EC and PC (propylene carbonate, $1:1$ , $\mathrm{Vol~}\%)$ with $5~\mathrm{vol\%}$ addition of FEC as electrolyte. For the synthesized $\mathrm{{Na_{3}V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3}}}$ half cells, the electrode was composed similar with Sb⊂CTHNs just replaced the cathode active materials (synthesized $\mathrm{Na_{3}V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3})}$ and the current collector (Al foil). The cutoff voltage of these half cells were measured from $2.5\mathrm{V}$ to $4.0\mathrm{V}$ . The devices of Na-ion full cells were also assembled with the Sb⊂CTHNs as anode electrode and the $\mathrm{Na_{3}V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3}}$ cathode as counter electrode directly with decreasing the mass loading of Sb⊂CTHNs in discs to $0.5{-}0.7\ \mathrm{mg\cm}^{-2}$ and increasing the mass loading of $\mathrm{{Na_{3}V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3}}}$ in discs to $6.5\mathrm{-}7.5~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The test voltage of these full cells was measured from $1.0\mathrm{V}$ to 3.3 V. The galvanostatic charge/discharge behaviors of Na-ion battery were measured similar with Li-ion battery. All specific capacity of these samples were measured by excluding the carbon in Sb⊂CTHNs. The preparation and electrochemical performance of Fe-MOF-Sb, Fe-Sb and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ for Li/Na-ion batteries were all similar with Sb⊂CTHNs electrode. Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS) were achieved with an electrochemical workstation in the frequency between $100~\mathrm{KHz}$ and $0.01\ \mathrm{Hz}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
The detail synthesis routes of Sb⊂CTHNs is illustrated in Scheme 1. As the previous research, the flexible Fe-MOF (MIL-88) nanorods were synthesized by a typical hydrothermal method with the $\mathrm{Fe^{+}}$ source of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and organic acid source of terephthalic acid dissolved in dimethyl formamide (DMF) solution. During the Fe-MOF formation process, the nucleation appeared gradually through the metal ion $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ as the core and linked by toughness organic chemical bonds. Then, mass nucleation gathered and the Fe-MOF nanorods with unique intrinsic porosity, high surface area and uniform dispersibility were obtained [25,29–32]. Second, the uniform pink Fe-MOF nanoparticles were delivered in absolute ethanol by sonication, and then TBOT and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ were added into the dispersion drop by drop respectively under magnetic stirring. Until the continuous hydrolysis reaction of TBOT, these colloid $\mathrm{Ti(OH)_{4}}$ like a protective shell homogeneously coated on the surface of needle-shaped Fe-MOF nanoparticles by hydrogen-bonding interactions and formed inorganic–organic compos­ ites with the typical core–shell-liked structure [33–37]. Then, the Fe$\mathrm{MOF@Ti(OH)}_{4}$ nanorods were annealed in $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ atmosphere in order to reduce the Fe-ion to well-crystal metal Fe and then the Fe nano­ particles encapsulated in MOFs-derived carbon and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow nano­ tubes (Fe⊂CTHNs) were synthesized. In the annealing reduction process, the organic acid were carbonized to carbon matrix and surrounded on the reductive metal Fe nanoparticles surface. In the final galvanic replacement process (the Fe⊂CTHNs dispersed in the absolute ethanol solution with ${\mathsf{s b C l}}_{3}$ salt), as the various standard electrode potentials $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Fe/Fe}}^{3+}=0.037\:\mathrm{V}^{\cdot}$ vs standard hydrogen electrode, SHE; $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Sb}/\mathrm{Sb}}^{3+}=0.241$ V vs SHE), the active Fe nanoparticles were oxidized to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and diffused from carbon matrix and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ network to solution gradually and deliv­ ered a faint yellow solution, simultaneously, the ${\mathsf{S b}}^{3+}$ ions were reduced to Sb nanoparticles by the natural electrode potential and encapsulated in carbon matrix and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ network, thus the uniform Sb⊂CTHNs were produced. As shown in Scheme 1 left, the well-crystallized Sb nano­ particles fully encapsulated in well-conductive carbon matrix and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ network which hold good mechanical properties could buffer the vol­ ume expansion during the $\mathrm{{Li^{+}/N a^{+}}}$ insertions/extractions, maintain the nanostructure stability and improve the electronic conductivity, pre­ sented excellent cycle stability and outstanding rate capability as Li/Naion batteries anode.
