Sb⊂CTHNs was also measured by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). As shown in Fig. 4b, the initial weak mass loss in TGA curve approximate $1.1\%$ from the room temperature to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which could be caused by the loss of water in sample. The next mass increase and subsequent loss of $14.2\%$ from 300 to $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ corresponded to the oxidation of Sb and carbon with the final materials of $\mathsf{S b}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ solid and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ gas [15,17,21]. The calculation of TGA indicated the carbon mass content in final uni­ form Sb⊂CTHNs was about $29.7\%$ , the content of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in Sb⊂CTHNs was about $5.76\%$ . Moreover, the formation carbon of the uniform Sb⊂CTHNs was further confirmed by Raman spectrum. Two defined bands at 1343.8 and $1580.8~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4c) which corresponded to disordered carbon (D-band) and ordered carbon (G-band), respectively. The intensity ratio of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}=0.83$ indicated the amorphous and relative disordered structures of the carbon matrix of Sb⊂CTHNs [21,40,41]. The nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms in Fig. 4d displayed a type III isotherm. Based on Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method, the uniform Sb⊂CTHNs hold mesoporous structure and a specific surface area of $126.9\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the graph inserted in Fig. 4d showed the pore size distribution of uniform Sb⊂CTHNs was mesoporous com­ posites with the wide pore size range of $10{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ . The mesoporous microstructure and high specific surface area could increase the contact of Sb⊂CTHNs electrode and electrolyte, decrease ions diffusion distance concurrently which were favorable for the electronic conductivity and exhibited higher capacity [6,8–14,20–24].
In order to further in-depth investigate the kinetics origin of the Sb⊂CTHNs with superior rate capability, the CV measurements was tested in half cell by various scan rates. Fig. 6a clearly presented the CV curves of Sb⊂CTHNs electrode at the scan rates from 0.1 to $1.0\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}$ with the similar shape. Normally, the degree of capacitive effect could be qualitatively analyzed depended on the relationship of measured current (i) and the scan rate (v) from the CV curves by the equation [10,43–45]:
$$
i=a\nu^{b}
$$
where the v stands for the scan rate, $a$ and $b$ both belong to adjustable parameters. According to the current research, the value of $b$ could be obtained by the slope of log i vs. log v from the liner fitting and the value was among 0.5 to 1.0 [10,44]. It was well known that the $b$ value of 0.5 represented the diffusion-controlled process and the value of 1.0 exhibited the surface capacitance-dominated process [10,43,44,46,47]. Herein, the $b$ -value of Sb⊂CTHNs electrode for cathodic peak and anodic peak were calculated to be 0.73 and 0.63 (the liner fitting slope of log i vs. log $\nu$ in Fig. 6b). The illogical $b$ -values of Sb⊂CTHNs indicated the favored capacitive kinetics displayed during the electrochemical re­ actions. Furthermore, the capacitive contributions of different scan rates could be determined quantitatively by the voltammetric sweep rate to the current response by the Equation:
$$
i(V)=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
where the $k_{1}\nu$ and $k_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ stand for the capacitive effect and diffusioncontrolled intersection, respectively. After simply transformed to:
$$
i(V)/\nu^{1/2}=k_{1}\nu^{1/2}+k_{2}
$$
the slope $k_{1}$ and section $k_{2}$ could be obtained from the plot of $i(V)/\nu^{1/\prime}$ $^2\nu{s}\ \nu^{1/2}$ . Subsequently, the capacitive effect contributions $i_{\mathrm{c}}\left(V\right)=k_{1}\nu$ from the total capacity could also be distinguished by the value of $k_{1}$ [43–47]. As a result, Fig. 6c presented the CV curves of the capacitive current relative to the combined current at the sweep rate of $0.6\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , which $81.8\%$ to total capacity as the capacitive contribution was quantified. According to this basic method, the various capacity ratios of the capacitive contribution at correspond scan rates were all identified. As shown in Fig. 6d, the capacitive contributions of the Sb⊂CTHNs electrode were increasing from $70.9\%$ , $74.7\%$ , $78.8\%$ , $81.8\%$ , $83.5\%$ to $84.3\%$ at the sweep rates of 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 and $1.0\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ , respectively. These high contributions of the capacitive storage may be dominated by the high surface area and the hierarchical structure of Sb⊂CTHNs which could increase the contact area with electrolyte and more easily formed double layer capacitance [32,42,43,47]. On the other hand, the nanocrystal Sb nanoparticles size shortened the diffusion distance of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ which also contribute to the capacitive reaction [45,47]. The increasing of capacitive effect directly determined the pseudoca­ pacitive capacity storage occupied a significant role of the total capacity and which were fully corresponded to the slope feature of galvanostatic profiles with the higher $b$ value and better kinetics, and corresponded to the excellent rate capability of the uniform Sb⊂CTHNs [44,47,48].
Furthermore, the detail electrochemical performances of the uniform Sb⊂CTHNs in the energy-storage devices were also investigated in full cells, which with the uniform Sb⊂CTHNs as anode and the reported $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ as cathode [49]. The characterization and excellent electro­ chemical performances of our homemade $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ cathode were exhibited in Fig. S10. The strange peaks in XRD pattern presented well crystallization. The characteristic flat plateaus in charging/discharging processes of $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ were located at $3.9{-}4.2\mathrm{~V~}$ , which was corre­ sponded to the research work [49]. The synthesized $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ cathode also presented a reversible capacity of $106.3\mathrm{\mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles and outstanding rate capability. When combined with the uniform Sb⊂CTHNs, the full cells of $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ were assembled (Schematic illustration of the full cell in Fig. 7a). Fig. 7b displayed the initial four CV curves of $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cells in a scan rate of $0.1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ between 2.0 and $4.0\mathrm{V}$ . The well-defined anodic and cathodic peak were presented at $3.3\mathrm{~V~}$ and $3.1~\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , respectively, which owing to the delithiation/lithiation voltages of $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ were around at $3.9\substack{-4.2\mathrm{~V~}}$ and the alloying/dealloying flat plateaus of Sb⊂CTHNs were $0.83\mathrm{~V}/1.1\mathrm{~V~}$ [9,12,13,48]. And the basically repeat curves in the following cycles indicated the good stability of $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cells. Fig. 7c exhibited the capacity-voltage curves of the full cells at $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the two typical charging/dis­ charging flat plateaus of $3.1/3.3\mathrm{V}$ which corresponded to the CV curves. While, the first Coulombic efficiency of the $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cells was only approximate $40\%$ , such huge capacity loss mainly due to the irreversible reactions of Sb⊂CTHNs anode, including the formation of SEI film, the decomposition of organic electrolyte and the interfacial Li storage, and the activated process of $\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ cathode [11–15,20–24,49]. The cycle stability of the $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cells were tested at the current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and delivered an initial capacity of 618.1 mA h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ after five prelithiumed cycles at a current density of $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 7d). Then a reversible capacity of $398.3\mathrm{\mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ was maintained after 100 cycles with high Coulombic efficiency of $98.8\%$ . The excellent rate capability at different current density of the full cells were also demanded for the electronic devices. As shown in Fig. 7e, the $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cells presented the reversible capacity of 637.4, 451.3, 390.3, 355.7, 311.6, 283.2, and $441.9\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 50, 100, 200, 500, 1000, $2000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and back to $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ . Moreover, the $\mathrm{SbcCTHNs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cells with supe­ rior cycle stability and high rate capability could light a light-emitting diode (LED, 2.0 V, 20 mA) and lasted for a long time (Fig. 7f), which verified directly that the novel Sb⊂CTHNs could be as one of the next anodes candidate for increasing the LIBs energy density.
